<article class="env-lemma" id="09VW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09VW">Lemma <span data-tag="09VW">25.6.3</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a site with equalizers and fibre products. Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a presheaf on $\mathcal{C}$. Let $K$ be a hypercovering of $\mathcal{G}$. Let $\mathcal{I}$ be an injective sheaf of abelian groups on $\mathcal{C}$. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \check{H}^ p(K, \mathcal{I}) = \left\{  \begin{matrix}  H^0(\mathcal{G}, \mathcal{I}) 

&  \text{if} 

&  p = 0 

\\  0 

&  \text{if} 

&  p > 0 

\end{matrix} \right.  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By (<a href="/tag/01GX" data-tag="01GX">25.5.2.1</a>) we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  s(\mathcal{F}(K)) = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{\textit{Ab}(\mathcal{C})}(s(\mathbf{Z}_{F(K)}^\# ), \mathcal{F})  \]
</div>
    <p>
       The complex $s(\mathbf{Z}_{F(K)}^\# )$ is quasi-isomorphic to $\mathbf{Z}_\mathcal {G}^\# $, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01GE" data-tag="01GE">25.4.4</a>. We conclude that if $\mathcal{I}$ is an injective abelian sheaf, then the complex $s(\mathcal{I}(K))$ is acyclic except possibly in degree $0$. In other words, we have $\check{H}^ i(K, \mathcal{I}) = 0$ for $i > 0$. Combined with Lemma <a href="/tag/09VV" data-tag="09VV">25.6.2</a> the lemma is proved. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>